
/*##########################################
    Sound Module - BTB Standard Library
############################################

    This sound module is mostly meant for games which is shown its design. Such as you can only take input from one microphone at a time.

    A platform independent module for playing audio.
    These are the general features:
        - Play WAV audio files (use external library for MP3 or OGG, they are complex)
        - Play multiple sounds in a 3D environment (needed in 3D games)
        - Capture audio from microphone.
        - Enumerating speaker and capture devices (headphones and microphones)
        - Play streamed audio. You create the audio, or receive it from a network connection (voice chat), or
          read it from a file and give it to this module and it will mix and play the audio accordingly.
    
    Proximity voice chat is possible by combining 3D sound mixing, capture microphone, and sending audio over a connection.
    You will need to implement transmission of audio over a connection though.

    This is supposed to be an improvement over WASAPI. If this module
    somewhow isn't then I have failed miserably. Please let me now
    if that is the case.

    At the bottom of this file you can find implementation decisions.
*/

/*  TODO:
        - Audio for Linux
        - Metrics on sound mixing performance. How much time is spent mixing sound and how many more sounds could you theoretically handle. Also how much memory is being used for sounds.
        - Audio processing. We can't mix sounds 1 second in advance because if you pick up an item then you will have
          to wait in worst case 1 second for the play call to be queued up and sound mixed in the next second.
          instead we mix sound every frame and fill up the buffer with 1 frame worth of audio data. The audio thread
          might run at 60 Hz while the game runs at 120 Hz. Audio thread might even be fine at 30 Hz.
        - Enumeration. At all times we'll store the strings and ids for the devices in sound_state. Every 0.5 seconds we'll
          call the enumerate device function from the backend API. This allows us to keep the backend calls on a
          seperate thread while from the developer's view they can just access the enumeration devices from sound_state as.
          They don't get affected by how different backend API's work. Also, we may need to access backend API from one
          thread only so keeping everything on the backend thread seems like a good idea.
*/

#import "Logger"
#import "File"
#if OS_WINDOWS
    #import "WASAPI"
    #import "Windows"
#endif
#import "String"
#import "Array"
#import "BucketArray"
#import "Math"
#import "Thread"

/*#################################
    PUBLIC USER LEVEL FUNCTIONS
##################################*/

enum SoundError {
    SOUND_SUCCESS,
    SOUND_FILE_NOT_FOUND,
    SOUND_UNSUPPORTED_FILE_FORMAT,
    SOUND_CORRUPTED_FILE,
}

#macro SOUND_INVALID_HANDLE 0

// A reference to a loaded sound.
// This reference owns the data associated with the sound (data loaded from file unless file is streamed)
struct SoundRef {
    handle: u64;
    fn valid() {
        return handle != SOUND_INVALID_HANDLE
    }
    fn cleanup() {
        // TODO: Destroy owned memory.
        // TODO: Destroy sound instances.
        handle = SOUND_INVALID_HANDLE
    }

    fn createInstance() -> SoundInstance {
        impl := sound_state.createSoundInstance({handle})
        return { impl.handle }
    }

    // Sound stream methods

    fn write(data: u8*, max_bytes: i32) -> i32 {
        sound := sound_state.getSoundRef(handle)
        
        // 5
        // 99 0 100
        if sound.buffer_head < sound.buffer_tail {
            bytes := max_bytes
            if max_bytes > sound.buffer_tail - sound.buffer_head {
                bytes = sound.buffer_tail - sound.buffer_head
            }
            if bytes == 0
                return bytes
            memcpy(sound.buffer.ptr + sound.buffer_head, data, bytes)
            sound.buffer_head = (sound.buffer_head + bytes) % sound.buffer.len
            if sound.buffer_head == sound.buffer_tail 
                sound.buffer_full = true
            return bytes
        }

        bytes := max_bytes
        if max_bytes > sound.buffer.len - sound.buffer_head {
            bytes = sound.buffer.len - sound.buffer_head
        }
        if bytes == 0
            return bytes
        memcpy(sound.buffer.ptr + sound.buffer_head, data, bytes)
        written := bytes
        sound.buffer_head = (sound.buffer_head + bytes) % sound.buffer.len
        if sound.buffer_head == sound.buffer_tail 
            sound.buffer_full = true

        if written == max_bytes
            return written

        bytes = max_bytes - written
        if sound.buffer_tail < bytes {
            bytes = sound.buffer_tail
        }
        if bytes == 0
            return written
        memcpy(sound.buffer.ptr, data + written, bytes)
        written += bytes
        sound.buffer_head = (sound.buffer_head + bytes) % sound.buffer.len
        if sound.buffer_head == sound.buffer_tail 
            sound.buffer_full = true
        return written
    }
    fn free_bytes_in_buffer() -> i32 {
        sound := sound_state.getSoundRef(handle)

        if sound.buffer_full
            return 0

        if sound.buffer_head < sound.buffer_tail {
            return sound.buffer_tail - sound.buffer_head
        }
        return sound.buffer_tail + sound.buffer.len - sound.buffer_head
    }
    fn max_bytes_in_buffer() -> i32 {
        sound := sound_state.getSoundRef(handle)
        return sound.buffer.len
    }
}

// An instance to sound
struct SoundInstance {
    handle: u64;
    fn valid() {
        return handle != SOUND_INVALID_HANDLE
    }
    fn cleanup() {
        // TODO: Destroy instance.
        handle = SOUND_INVALID_HANDLE
    }

    fn play() {
        // log("Play ", handle)
        instance := sound_state.getSoundInstance(handle)
        instance.playing = true
    }
    fn stop() {
        // log("Stop ", handle)
        instance := sound_state.getSoundInstance(handle)
        instance.playing = false
    }
    fn set_timestamp(timestamp: f64) {
        // log("set timestamp ", timestamp)
        instance := sound_state.getSoundInstance(handle)
        sound := sound_state.getSoundRef(instance.sound_ref_handle)
        instance.current_frame = timestamp * sound.wav.sample_rate
    }
    fn setVolume(volume: f32) {
        // log("Set volume ", volume)
    }
    fn isPlaying() -> bool {
        instance := sound_state.getSoundInstance(handle)
        return instance.playing
    }
}

// This function is mainly meant for testing.
fn ProcessSoundForNSeconds(time: f64) {
    // Can't do ProcessSleep because sound is processed 1 frame at a time
    global last_time: i64 = 0
    if last_time == 0
        last_time = StartMeasure()

    while time > 0.0 {
        now := StartMeasure()
        delta := DiffMeasure(now - last_time)
        last_time = now
        // log(time," ", delta)
        time -= delta

        ProcessSound()
        ProcessSleep(0.001)
    }
}

fn ListInputAudioDevices(list: Array<AudioDeviceInfo>*) {
    Assert(sound_state)
    list.clear()
    // TODO: Implement input devices
    Assert(false)
}
fn ListOutputAudioDevices(list: Array<AudioDeviceInfo>*) {
    Assert(sound_state)

    sound_state.audio_backend.listAudioDevices(list, exclude_input_devices=true)
}
fn ChangeOutputAudioDevice(id: u64) -> bool {
    Assert(sound_state)

    // "queue" the output device change action, handled when we process sound (potentially on another thread)
    sound_state.selected_output_device_id = id;
    return false
}

// @param format Format of internal buffer and the amount of samples it can hold. This how often you have to consume the buffer before it is filled up. Sample rate of 48 0000, 32-bit float, and stereo is recommended.
// @param frame_count Number of frames (each frame has a number of samples equal to the number of channels)
fn InitInputAudioBuffer(size_of_buffer_in_seconds: f32, format: AudioFormat*) {
    Assert(sound_state)

    // can't initialize audio buffer twice
    Assert(!sound_state.input_buffer.ptr)

    sound_state.audio_backend.init_default_input_device()

    size := sound_state.audio_backend.input_format.byte_size_from_duration(size_of_buffer_in_seconds)

    sound_state.capture_input_audio = true
    sound_state.input_buffer.ptr = Allocate(size)
    Assert(sound_state.input_buffer.ptr)
    sound_state.input_buffer.len = size
    sound_state.input_buffer_head = 0
    sound_state.input_buffer_tail = 0;

    *format = sound_state.audio_backend.input_format
}

// @return Bytes in internal input buffer. If bytes is equal to max audio buffer size then it is likely you lost samples because you weren't consuming the buffer fast enough.
fn BytesInInputAudioBuffer() -> i32 {
    Assert(sound_state)

    if !sound_state.capture_input_audio
        return 0

    if sound_state.input_buffer_head > sound_state.input_buffer_tail {
        return sound_state.input_buffer_head - sound_state.input_buffer_tail
    }
    return sound_state.input_buffer_tail + sound_state.input_buffer.len - sound_state.input_buffer_head
}

// @param user_buffer Audio data is moved from internal buffer to user buffer.
// @param max_size Maximum bytes to consume. Must be divisible by frame size (sound_state.sound_state.audio_backend.get_frame_size())
// @return Consumed bytes. Always divisible by frame size of input/capture audio format.
fn ConsumeInputAudioBuffer(user_buffer: u8*, max_size: i32) -> i32 {
    Assert(sound_state)
    Assert(max_size != 0)
    Assert(0 == max_size % sound_state.audio_backend.input_format.get_frame_size())

    if !sound_state.capture_input_audio
        return 0

    if !sound_state.input_buffer_full && sound_state.input_buffer_tail == sound_state.input_buffer_head
        return 0

    if sound_state.input_buffer_tail < sound_state.input_buffer_head {
        bytes := max_size
        if sound_state.input_buffer_head - sound_state.input_buffer_tail < max_size {
            bytes = sound_state.input_buffer_head - sound_state.input_buffer_tail
        }
        memcpy(user_buffer, sound_state.input_buffer.ptr + sound_state.input_buffer_tail, bytes)
        sound_state.input_buffer_tail += bytes
        Assert(0 == bytes % sound_state.audio_backend.input_format.get_frame_size())
        return bytes
    }
    bytes := max_size
    if sound_state.input_buffer.len - sound_state.input_buffer_tail < max_size {
        bytes = sound_state.input_buffer.len - sound_state.input_buffer_tail
    }
    memcpy(user_buffer, sound_state.input_buffer.ptr + sound_state.input_buffer_tail, bytes)
    sound_state.input_buffer_tail = (sound_state.input_buffer_tail + bytes) % sound_state.input_buffer.len
    sound_state.input_buffer_full = false
    if bytes == max_size {
        Assert(0 == bytes % sound_state.audio_backend.input_format.get_frame_size())
        return bytes
    }

    read_bytes := bytes
    bytes = max_size - read_bytes

    if sound_state.input_buffer_head < bytes {
        bytes = sound_state.input_buffer_head
    }

    memcpy(user_buffer + read_bytes, sound_state.input_buffer.ptr, bytes)
    sound_state.input_buffer_tail = (sound_state.input_buffer_tail + bytes) % sound_state.input_buffer.len
    read_bytes += bytes
    Assert(0 == bytes % sound_state.audio_backend.input_format.get_frame_size())
    return read_bytes
}

enum AudioSampleFormat {
    AUDIO_8BIT_PCM,
    AUDIO_16BIT_PCM,
    AUDIO_32BIT_PCM,
    AUDIO_32BIT_FLOAT,
}
struct AudioFormat {
    sample_rate: i32; // 48 0000 Hz
    stereo: bool; // false - Mono, true - Stereo
    sample_format: AudioSampleFormat; // AUDIO_32BIT_FLOAT

    fn init(_sample_rate: i32, channels: i32, bits_per_sample: i32, is_float: bool) {
        sample_rate = _sample_rate
        stereo = channels == 2
        Assert(channels == 1 || channels == 2)
        if !is_float {
            if bits_per_sample == 32 {
                sample_format = AUDIO_32BIT_PCM
            } else if bits_per_sample == 16 {
                sample_format = AUDIO_16BIT_PCM
            }  else if bits_per_sample == 8 {
                sample_format = AUDIO_8BIT_PCM
            } else {
                Assert(!is_float && (bits_per_sample == 8 || bits_per_sample == 16 || bits_per_sample == 32))
            }
        } else {
            if bits_per_sample == 32 {
                sample_format = AUDIO_32BIT_FLOAT
            } else {
                Assert(is_float && bits_per_sample == 32)
            }
        }
    }

    fn frames_from_duration(time: f32) -> i32 {
        return sample_rate * time
    }
    fn byte_size_from_duration(time: f32) -> i32 {
        // buffer size must be a multiple of frame size, we must cast float before multiplying frame size
        return get_frame_size() * cast<i32>(sample_rate * time)
    }
    fn get_channels() -> i32 {
        if stereo
            return 2
        return 1
    }
    fn get_bits_per_sample() -> i32 {
        switch sample_format {
            case AUDIO_8BIT_PCM:
                return 8
            case AUDIO_16BIT_PCM:
                return 16
            case AUDIO_32BIT_PCM:
            case AUDIO_32BIT_FLOAT:
                return 32
        }
        return -1
    }
    fn get_frame_size() -> i32 {
        return (get_bits_per_sample()/8) * get_channels()
    }
}

/*###############################
    IMPLEMENTATION SPECIFIC
###############################*/

struct SoundRef_impl {
    handle: u64; // handle is 64-bit in case we ever want it to represent a pointer
    name: StringBuilder; // path to audio file or user defined name when using LoadSoundFromRawFileData
    bucket_index: i32;

    // TODO: Add stream data and fields for it
    wav: WAVFile*;
    total_frames: i32;

    // stream buffer and format when streaming
    format: AudioFormat;
    buffer: u8[]; // circular buffer
    buffer_head: i32;
    buffer_tail: i32;
    buffer_full: bool;

    fn available_bytes_in_buffer() -> i32 {
        if buffer_full
            return buffer.len

        if buffer_tail <= buffer_head {
            return buffer_head - buffer_tail
        }
        return buffer_head + buffer.len - buffer_tail
    }
    fn max_bytes_in_buffer() -> i32 {
        return buffer.len
    }
}
struct SoundInstance_impl {
    handle: u64;
    sound_ref_handle: u64;
    bucket_index: i32;

    playing: bool;
    current_frame: i32; // number of frames written to audio device buffer (not that the sample may not have been played by audio backend yet)

    // TODO: pos: vec3;
}

// Manager of all resources such as:
//   references, streams, instances, memory, file handles, and threads.
struct SoundState {
    refs: BucketArray<SoundRef_impl>;
    instances: BucketArray<SoundInstance_impl>;
    m_next_handle: u64;
    audio_backend: AudioBackend;

    last_time: i64;
    global_volume: f32 = 1.0;

    selected_output_device_id: u64;
    current_output_device_id: u64;

    // circular input buffer, initialized by InitInputAudioBuffer
    capture_input_audio: bool;
    input_buffer: u8[];
    input_buffer_head: i32;
    input_buffer_tail: i32;
    input_buffer_full: i32;

    fn init() {
        refs.init(64)
        instances.init(64)
        m_next_handle = 1
    } 
    fn cleanup() {
        refs.cleanup()
        instances.cleanup()
        audio_backend.cleanup()
    }

    // @return Always returns a valid pointer (asserts if something fails)
    fn createSoundRef(name: char[]) -> SoundRef_impl* {
        impl, index := refs.add(null)
        Assert(impl)
        impl.bucket_index = index
        impl.handle = nextHandle()
        impl.name.append(name)
        return impl
    }

    fn getSoundRef(handle: u64) -> SoundRef_impl* {
        for refs {
            if it.handle == handle
                return it
        }
        return null
    }

    // 0.0 - maxfloat?
    fn setGlobalVolume(volume: f32) {
        global_volume = volume
    }

    // @return Always returns a valid pointer (asserts if something fails)
    fn createSoundInstance(ref: SoundRef) -> SoundInstance_impl* {
        impl, index := instances.add(null)
        Assert(impl)
        impl.bucket_index = index
        impl.handle = nextHandle()
        impl.sound_ref_handle = ref.handle
        return impl
    }

    fn getSoundInstance(handle: u64) -> SoundInstance_impl* {
        for instances {
            if it.handle == handle
                return it
        }
        return null
    }

    fn nextHandle() -> u64 {
        return m_next_handle++
    }
}
struct AudioDeviceInfo {
    id: u64;
    name: StringBuilder;
    is_default: bool;
    is_input_device: bool; // input means microphone
}
global sound_state: SoundState*

// TODO: Flags on whether to create a thread or if user should call a function to do sound processing.
fn InitSoundState() {
    Assert(!sound_state)

    sound_state = Allocate(sizeof(SoundState))
    construct(sound_state)

    // TODO: We should not initialize default device by default.
    //   An application may have a settings, config or cache file where the audio device used last time
    //   the application was running is stored. If so they want to use that device instead of the default.
    sound_state.audio_backend.init_default_device()
}

fn DestroySoundState() {
    Assert(sound_state)

    sound_state.cleanup()
    Free(sound_state, sizeof(SoundState))
    sound_state = null
}
global sound_cycles: i64
global sound_cycles_max: i64
global sound_frames: i64

fn ProcessSound() {
    Assert(sound_state)

    if sound_state.selected_output_device_id != sound_state.current_output_device_id {
        // next_device_id
        sound_state.audio_backend.switchAudioDevice(sound_state.selected_output_device_id)
    }

    start_time := StartMeasure()


    // Check the state of the buffer. Do we need to fill it up.

    /* Concerns:
        If we have 5 sounds with 1-5 second durations then we can't fill up the buffer with all those sounds
        because we might want to add new sounds the next frame. So we'll process the sound for this frame.
    */

    if sound_state.last_time == 0 {
        // first time processing sound
        sound_state.last_time = StartMeasure()
    }
    now := StartMeasure()
    delta := DiffMeasure(now - sound_state.last_time)
    sound_state.last_time = now


    remaining_frames := sound_state.audio_backend.remainingFrames()

    // Generally speaking, through experiments. process_rate should be double the frequency at which we call ProcessSound.
    // Holding two game frames worth of audio keeps us safe.
    // process_rate: f64 = 0.020 // 20 ms (We expect to run ProcessSound at 60 Hz, 60 times per second, this would be 16.66 ms but we give it some leeway)
    process_rate: f64 = 4 * 0.016 // 20 ms (We expect to run ProcessSound at 60 Hz, 60 times per second, this would be 16.66 ms but we give it some leeway)

    wanted_frames_in_buffer: i32 = process_rate * sound_state.audio_backend.wave_format.Format.nSamplesPerSec

    frames_to_write := wanted_frames_in_buffer - remaining_frames

    if(remaining_frames == 0) {
        // Show delta information and so on...
        // log("Sound process engine can't keep up!")
    }
    // log("delta ", delta, " ", frames_to_write)
    // log("delta", delta, " ", remaining_frames,"/",wanted_frames_in_buffer)
    // log("delta", delta, " ", remaining_frames,"/",wanted_frames_in_buffer)

    if frames_to_write == 0 {
        // log("(nothing to write), delta ", delta)
    }

    // TODO: Measure audio processing time.

    // TODO: If no audio is playing then don't acquire buffer.

    // NOTE: We expect the audio stream to be 32-bit float. We will handle others in the future.
    buffer: f32* = cast<f32*>sound_state.audio_backend.acquireBuffer(frames_to_write)
    buffer_len := frames_to_write * 2 * 4 // channels * 32-bit float PCM
    memzero(buffer,buffer_len)
    
    // TODO: Read about GetBuffer and ReleaseBuffer and Initialize AudioClient for a correct implementation where
    //   Errors are handled. For example we write to slowly to the buffer.

    // TODO: Parse WAV sound data

    // log("####### DELTA ", delta)
    // log("Remaining frames ", remaining_frames, " (",wanted_frames_in_buffer,")")
    // log("volume ", sound_state.global_volume)
    // log("frames_to_write ", frames_to_write)

    // TODO: Check thread safety
    for instance: sound_state.instances {
        sound := sound_state.getSoundRef(instance.sound_ref_handle)

        if !instance.playing
            continue

        // TODO: 24-bit support?
        form := sound_state.audio_backend.output_format
        
        if sound.wav {
            wav := sound.wav
            
            out_written, in_written := MixAudio(buffer, &form, wav.data + instance.current_frame * wav.format.get_frame_size(), &wav.format, frames_to_write, sound.total_frames - instance.current_frame , sound_state.global_volume)
            instance.current_frame += in_written

            if instance.current_frame >= sound.total_frames {
                instance.playing = false
            }
        } else {
            // Sound stream
            // For now we only handle same sample rate
            // Assert(sound.format.sample_rate == sound_state.audio_backend.wave_format.Format.nSamplesPerSec)

            // log("frames to write ", frames_to_write, ", available ", sound.available_bytes_in_buffer())
            
            // We should not check if enough bytes is in the sound stream and if not don't play any off them.
            // We should play whatever we havea in the buffer and if it's not enough then it's the users fault
            // that they didn't provide enough data in the stream.
            // if frames_to_write > sound.available_bytes_in_buffer() { // TODO: This comparison assumes the sample rates are the same.
            //     log("Skippers ", frames_to_write, " ava ", sound.available_bytes_in_buffer())
            //     // sound stream don't have enough data
            //     continue
            // }
            state := sound_state

            reverse_sample_ratio: f32 = cast<f32>sound_state.audio_backend.output_format.sample_rate / cast<f32>sound.format.sample_rate

            my_frames_to_write: i32 = reverse_sample_ratio * (sound.available_bytes_in_buffer() / sound.format.get_frame_size())
            // TODO: Assumes the same sample rate on the sound stream and out audio device
            if my_frames_to_write > frames_to_write
                my_frames_to_write = frames_to_write

            // Assert(sound.format.sample_format == AUDIO_32BIT_FLOAT)

            #macro CHECK_DIV(A,B) Assert(A/B*B == A)

            // log("tail ", sound.buffer_tail, ", head ", sound.buffer_head)
            if sound.buffer_tail < sound.buffer_head {
                // nocheckin TODO: Threading issues when user writes and modifies the head. available_bytes_in_buffer is affected.
                CHECK_DIV(sound.buffer_head - sound.buffer_tail, sound.format.get_frame_size())
                MixAudio(buffer, &form, sound.buffer.ptr + sound.buffer_tail, &sound.format, my_frames_to_write, (sound.buffer_head - sound.buffer_tail) / sound.format.get_frame_size(), sound_state.global_volume)
            } else {
                // This code assumes sample rates are the same, you will need to multiple sample rate ratio to max_in_frames to support different sample rates.
                CHECK_DIV(sound.buffer.len - sound.buffer_tail, sound.format.get_frame_size())
                out_written, in_written := MixAudio(buffer, &form, sound.buffer.ptr + sound.buffer_tail, &sound.format, my_frames_to_write, (sound.buffer.len - sound.buffer_tail) / sound.format.get_frame_size(), sound_state.global_volume)

                // log(" ", my_frames_to_write, " written ", out_written, " ", in_written)
                
                // We need two calls to MixAudio when going around the sound stream's circular buffer
                if out_written != my_frames_to_write {
                    // we shall write more frames
                    CHECK_DIV(sound.buffer_head, sound.format.get_frame_size())
                    MixAudio(buffer + out_written * form.get_frame_size(), &form, sound.buffer.ptr, &sound.format, my_frames_to_write - out_written, sound.buffer_head / sound.format.get_frame_size(), sound_state.global_volume)
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // Advance tail of all sound streams in one go. This should be done after instances have played from the 
    // sound streams.
    for sound: sound_state.refs {
        if !sound.buffer.ptr
            continue

        sample_ratio: f32 = cast<f32>sound.format.sample_rate / cast<f32>sound_state.audio_backend.output_format.sample_rate
        // sound frames, not output buffer frames because they may have different sample rates and
        // we need to clearly differentiate them
        my_sound_frames_to_write := sound.available_bytes_in_buffer() / sound.format.get_frame_size()
        // TODO: Assumes the same sample rate on the sound stream and out audio device
        if my_sound_frames_to_write > frames_to_write * sample_ratio
            my_sound_frames_to_write = frames_to_write * sample_ratio
        
        // nocheckin TODO: we add frames_to_write which assumes sample rates are the same

        // log("Move tail ", sound.buffer_tail, " + ", my_sound_frames_to_write, " = ",(sound.buffer_tail + my_sound_frames_to_write) % sound.buffer.len, ", avail bytes ",sound.available_bytes_in_buffer())
        log("INFO ", sound.buffer_tail, " ", sound.buffer_head, " ", sound.buffer_full)
        sound.buffer_tail = (sound.buffer_tail + my_sound_frames_to_write * sound.format.get_frame_size()) % sound.buffer.len
        if sound.buffer_head != sound.buffer_tail 
            sound.buffer_full = false
     }

    sound_state.audio_backend.releaseBuffer(frames_to_write)

    if sound_state.capture_input_audio {
        // TODO: We assume stereo 32-bit float 48000 Hz
        capture := sound_state.audio_backend.captureService
        frames: u32
        res: i32
        while {
            res = capture.vtable.GetNextPacketSize(capture, &frames)
            CHECK_HR

            if frames == 0
                break

            packet_bytes := cast<i32>frames * sound_state.audio_backend.input_format.get_frame_size()

            // Our capture buffer is too small (size specified with InitInputBuffer).
            // We have to read whole packets.
            Assert(packet_bytes < sound_state.input_buffer.len)

            free_bytes := 0
            if sound_state.input_buffer_full {
                // no free bytes
            } else if sound_state.input_buffer_head < sound_state.input_buffer_tail {
                free_bytes = sound_state.input_buffer_tail - sound_state.input_buffer_head
            } else {
                free_bytes = sound_state.input_buffer.len - sound_state.input_buffer_head + sound_state.input_buffer_tail
            }
            // log("INPUT BUFFER ", sound_state.input_buffer_head, " ", sound_state.input_buffer_tail, " ", sound_state.input_buffer.len)

            // A thought about different kind of consumer/producer-cycle. Comment should exist elsewhere but here it is.
            //   We may want a circular buffer but not
            //   consumer/producer-style. We want to capture audio into the circular buffer continously writing
            //   at and incrementing head while overwriting whatever data is there. Then user of the sound module reads
            //   bytes from the tail at the same pace. If they read slower or faster then audio is messed up.
            //   With this approach we need some sort of function to know how many bytes we should read.
            //   With the consumer/producer style we had a function that told us how many free or used bytes we had.
            //   could just consume all of those and transfer over network for voice chat.
            //   
            //  The reason we want this new approach is that the buffer never fills up. We always have fresh data.
            //  With a bigger buffer we would have less fresh audio data but more room for inconsistent reads with
            //  cost of higher latency.


            // Whether we have free bytes in the buffer or not we will always consume bytes from
            // the capture service. If the whole audio system is working correctly we should consume and
            // mix or send voice audio to headphones or over the network at the same speed. We should
            // always have free bytes available.

            // TODO: With that said, we don't have to skip the whole packet. We could transfer some samples in the packet at least altough if we're already skipping parts of packets something is wrong so maybe it's best to skip whole packets so we notice issues in the sound more clearly?

            data: u8*
            flags: u32
            res = capture.vtable.GetBuffer(capture, &data, &frames, &flags, null, null)
            CHECK_HR
            
            if packet_bytes <= free_bytes {
                if sound_state.input_buffer_head < sound_state.input_buffer_tail {
                    memcpy(sound_state.input_buffer.ptr + sound_state.input_buffer_head, data, packet_bytes)
                    sound_state.input_buffer_head = (sound_state.input_buffer_head + packet_bytes) % sound_state.input_buffer.len
                    if sound_state.input_buffer_head == sound_state.input_buffer_tail
                        sound_state.input_buffer_full = true
                } else {
                    bytes := packet_bytes
                    if bytes > sound_state.input_buffer.len - sound_state.input_buffer_head {
                        bytes = sound_state.input_buffer.len - sound_state.input_buffer_head
                    }
                    memcpy(sound_state.input_buffer.ptr + sound_state.input_buffer_head, data, bytes)
                    sound_state.input_buffer_head = (sound_state.input_buffer_head + bytes) % sound_state.input_buffer.len

                    written := bytes
                    
                    bytes = packet_bytes - written // rest of packet bytes will fit, we checked already no need to clamp
                    memcpy(sound_state.input_buffer.ptr, data + written, bytes)
                    sound_state.input_buffer_head = (sound_state.input_buffer_head + bytes) % sound_state.input_buffer.len

                    if sound_state.input_buffer_head == sound_state.input_buffer_tail
                        sound_state.input_buffer_full = true
                }
            } else {
                // log("Skipping captured audio packet of ", packet_bytes, " bytes, input buffer has no space (",free_bytes," bytes)")
            }

            res = capture.vtable.ReleaseBuffer(capture, frames)
            CHECK_HR
        }
    }

    end_time := StartMeasure()
    sound_cycles+=end_time - start_time
    sound_frames+=1
    if end_time - start_time > sound_cycles_max && sound_frames > 5 // throw away first few
        sound_cycles_max = end_time - start_time
    tim := DiffMeasure(end_time - start_time)
    // log("Frame time ", tim*1000, " ms")
}

// returns written source frames, if frames_to_write is more than max frames in the in buffer then written source frames
// may be considerably less than what you might expect. This is fine, it means in buffer or the in sound stopped.
// returned written source frames will not be the same as frames_to_write if the in and out buffer has different sample rates.
fn MixAudio(out_buffer: f32*, out_format: AudioFormat*, in_buffer: u8*, in_format: AudioFormat*, frames_to_write: i32, in_max_frames: i32, volume: f32) -> i32, i32 {
    Assert(out_format.sample_format == AUDIO_32BIT_FLOAT)
    Assert(out_format.stereo)

    sample_ratio: f32 = cast<f32>in_format.sample_rate / cast<f32>out_format.sample_rate
    // bits_per_sample: i32 = in_format.get_bits_per_sample()
    channels: i32 = in_format.get_channels()
    audio_format: AudioSampleFormat = in_format.sample_format

    src_32f: f32*
    src_32 : i32*
    src_16 : i16*
    src_8  : u8* 

    switch in_format.sample_format {
        case AUDIO_32BIT_FLOAT:
            src_32f = cast<f32*>in_buffer
        case AUDIO_16BIT_PCM:
            src_16  = cast<i16*>in_buffer
        case AUDIO_32BIT_PCM:
            src_32  = cast<i32*>in_buffer
        case AUDIO_8BIT_PCM:
            src_8   = cast<u8* >in_buffer
    }

    // nocheckin TODO: I think we're missing to write one sample here. If you listen closely to 16 khz audio there is a faint noise or stutter in the sound. It's probably due to a missing sample.

    src_sample_index0: i32
    nr: i32 = 0
    while nr < frames_to_write {
        src_sample_index: f32 = nr * sample_ratio
        src_sample_index0 = src_sample_index
        defer nr++

        fraction: f32 = src_sample_index - src_sample_index0

        // log("d ", src_sample_index0, " ", fraction)

        if src_sample_index0 + 1 >= in_max_frames {
            break
        }

        out_sample0: f32
        out_sample1: f32
        if audio_format == AUDIO_8BIT_PCM && channels == 1 {
            out_sample0 = ((cast<i32>src_8[src_sample_index0] - 128) / 128.0) * (1.0 - fraction) + ((cast<i32>src_8[src_sample_index0 + 1] - 128) / 128.0) * fraction
            out_sample1 = out_sample0

        } else if audio_format == AUDIO_8BIT_PCM && channels == 2 {
            out_sample0 := ((cast<i32>src_8[2*src_sample_index0    ] - 128) / 128.0) * (1.0 - fraction) + ((cast<i32>src_8[2*(src_sample_index0 + 1)    ] - 128) / 128.0) * fraction
            out_sample1 := ((cast<i32>src_8[2*src_sample_index0 + 1] - 128) / 128.0) * (1.0 - fraction) + ((cast<i32>src_8[2*(src_sample_index0 + 1) + 1] - 128) / 128.0) * fraction
        
        } else if audio_format == AUDIO_16BIT_PCM && channels == 1 {
            out_sample0 = ((cast<i32>src_16[src_sample_index0]) / 32768.0) * (1.0 - fraction) + ((cast<i32>src_16[src_sample_index0 + 1]) / 32768.0) * fraction
            out_sample1 = out_sample0
        
        } else if audio_format == AUDIO_16BIT_PCM && channels == 2 {
            out_sample0 = ((cast<i32>src_16[2*src_sample_index0    ]) / 32768.0) * (1.0 - fraction) + ((cast<i32>src_16[2*(src_sample_index0 + 1)    ]) / 32768.0) * fraction
            out_sample1 = ((cast<i32>src_16[2*src_sample_index0 + 1]) / 32768.0) * (1.0 - fraction) + ((cast<i32>src_16[2*(src_sample_index0 + 1) + 1]) / 32768.0) * fraction
        
        } else if audio_format == AUDIO_32BIT_PCM && channels == 1 {
            out_sample0 = ((cast<i32>src_32[src_sample_index0]) / 2147483648.0) * (1.0 - fraction) + ((cast<i32>src_32[src_sample_index0 + 1]) / 2147483648.0) * fraction
            out_sample1 = out_sample0
        
        } else if audio_format == AUDIO_32BIT_PCM && channels == 2 {
            out_sample0 = ((cast<i32>src_32[2*src_sample_index0    ]) / 2147483648.0) * (1.0 - fraction) + ((cast<i32>src_32[2*(src_sample_index0 + 1)    ]) / 2147483648.0) * fraction
            out_sample1 = ((cast<i32>src_32[2*src_sample_index0 + 1]) / 2147483648.0) * (1.0 - fraction) + ((cast<i32>src_32[2*(src_sample_index0 + 1) + 1]) / 2147483648.0) * fraction
        
        } else if audio_format == AUDIO_32BIT_FLOAT && channels == 1 {
            out_sample0 = src_32f[src_sample_index0] * (1.0 - fraction) + src_32f[src_sample_index0 + 1] * fraction
            out_sample1 = out_sample0
        
        } else if audio_format == AUDIO_32BIT_FLOAT && channels == 2 {
            out_sample0 = src_32f[2*src_sample_index0    ] * (1.0 - fraction) + src_32f[2*(src_sample_index0 + 1)    ] * fraction
            out_sample1 = src_32f[2*src_sample_index0 + 1] * (1.0 - fraction) + src_32f[2*(src_sample_index0 + 1) + 1] * fraction
        }

        out_buffer[2*nr    ] += out_sample0 * volume
        out_buffer[2*nr + 1] += out_sample1 * volume
    }
    return nr, src_sample_index0 + 1
}


struct InternalAudioDevice {
    id: u64;
    backend_id: StringBuilder; // uuid on windows
}
struct AudioBackend {
    // platform-independent
    output_format: AudioFormat;
    input_format: AudioFormat;

    // platform specific
    enumerator: IMMDeviceEnumerator*;
    device: IMMDevice*;
    audioClient: IAudioClient*;
    wave_format: WAVEFORMATEXTENSIBLE*;
    renderService: IAudioRenderClient*;

    input_wave_format: WAVEFORMATEXTENSIBLE*;
    input_device: IMMDevice*;
    input_audioClient: IAudioClient*;
    captureService: IAudioCaptureClient*;

    mutex: Mutex;
    internalAudioDevices: Array<InternalAudioDevice>;
    m_next_device_id: u64 = 1;

    buffer_duration_100ns: i32 = 80_000_00; // 80 ms

    fn next_device_id() -> u64 {
        return m_next_device_id++
    }

    fn init_default_device() {
        #macro CHECK_HR if res != 0 { log("HR BAD ", cast_unsafe<WASAPI_Error*>&res); Assert(false) }

        // #macro WASLOG(...) log(...)
        #macro WASLOG(...) #endmacro


        res: HRESULT

        res = CoInitialize(null)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass CoInitialize")
        
        
        res = CoCreateInstance(&MMDeviceEnumerator_GUID, null, cast<u32>CLSCTX_ALL, &IMMDeviceEnumerator_GUID, &enumerator)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass CoCreateInstance")

        // collection: IMMDeviceCollection*

        // res = enumerator.vtable.EnumAudioEndpoints(enumerator, eRender, DEVICE_STATE_ACTIVE, &collection)
        // CHECK_HR
        // log("pass EnumAudioEndpoints")

        // count: u32
        // res = collection.vtable.GetCount(collection, &count)
        // CHECK_HR
        // // log("pass GetCount")
        // log(count)

        // for 0..count {
        //     endpoint: IMMDevice*
        //     props: IPropertyStore*
        //     res = collection.vtable.Item(collection, nr, &endpoint)
        //     CHECK_HR
        //     // log("pass Item ",nr)

        //     res = endpoint.vtable.OpenPropertyStore(endpoint, STGM_READ, &props)
        //     CHECK_HR
        //     // log("pass OpenPropertyStore")


        //     ch: char[50] // extra buffer in case PROPVARIANT is defined to small
        //     prop_name: PROPVARIANT
            

        //     res = props.vtable.GetValue(props, &DEVPKEY_Device_FriendlyName, &prop_name)
        //     CHECK_HR
        //     // log("pass GetValue")

        //     name: StringBuilder;
        //     ParseWideCharString(cast<u16*>cast<void*>prop_name.union_data, &name)

        //     log("Device ",nr, " : ", name)

        //     props.vtable.Release(props)
        //     endpoint.vtable.Release(endpoint)
        // }

        res = enumerator.vtable.GetDefaultAudioEndpoint(enumerator, eRender, eConsole, &device)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass GetDefaultAudioEndpoint")

        // TODO: Do something if default audio device isn't available. We can't play sound in this case.

        init_device()        
    }
    fn init_device() {
        res: i32;

        res = device.vtable.Activate(device, &IAudioClient_GUID, CLSCTX_ALL, null, &audioClient)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass device.Activate")

        res = audioClient.vtable.GetMixFormat(audioClient, cast<WAVEFORMATEX**>&wave_format)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass audioClient.GetMixFormat")
        // log("WAVE format:")
        // std_print(wave_format, pretty = true)

        res = audioClient.vtable.Initialize(audioClient, AUDCLNT_SHAREMODE_SHARED, 0, buffer_duration_100ns, 0,  cast<WAVEFORMATEX*>wave_format, null)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass audioClient.Initialize")

        res = audioClient.vtable.GetService(audioClient, &IAudioRenderClient_GUID, &renderService)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass audioClient.GetService")

        audioClient.vtable.Start(audioClient)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass audioClient.Start")

        // TODO: Extinsible format may have multiple channels, more than SPEAKER_FRONT_LEFT and SPEAKER_FRONT_RIGHT.
        //   Those are the only channels we support so we should zero the other ones (if using float, if using 16-bit then set to 2^15).

        Assert(wave_format.Format.nChannels == 1 || wave_format.Format.nChannels == 2)

        is_float := equal(cast<char*>&wave_format.SubFormat, cast<char*>&KSDATAFORMAT_SUBTYPE_IEEE_FLOAT, 16)
        Assert(is_float || equal(cast<char*>&wave_format.SubFormat, cast<char*>&KSDATAFORMAT_SUBTYPE_PCM, 16))

        output_format.init(wave_format.Format.nSamplesPerSec, wave_format.Format.nChannels, wave_format.Format.wBitsPerSample, is_float)

        // TODO: Handle 16-bit and 32-bit integer?
        // Assert(equal(cast<char*>&wave_format.SubFormat, cast<char*>&KSDATAFORMAT_SUBTYPE_IEEE_FLOAT, 16))
        // Assert(wave_format.Format.nChannels == 2)
        // Assert(wave_format.Format.wBitsPerSample == 32)
        // Assert(equal(cast<char*>&expected_format.SubFormat, cast<char*>&KSDATAFORMAT_SUBTYPE_PCM, 16))
    }

    fn init_default_input_device() {
        res: i32
        res = enumerator.vtable.GetDefaultAudioEndpoint(enumerator, eCapture, eConsole, &input_device)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass GetDefaultAudioEndpoint")

        // TODO: Do something if default audio device isn't available. We can't play sound in this case.

        res = input_device.vtable.Activate(input_device, &IAudioClient_GUID, CLSCTX_ALL, null, &input_audioClient)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass device.Activate")

        res = input_audioClient.vtable.GetMixFormat(input_audioClient, cast<WAVEFORMATEX**>&input_wave_format)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass audioClient.GetMixFormat")
        // log("WAVE format:")
        // std_print(input_wave_format, pretty = true)
        // log(input_wave_format)

        res = input_audioClient.vtable.Initialize(input_audioClient, AUDCLNT_SHAREMODE_SHARED, 0, buffer_duration_100ns, 0,  cast<WAVEFORMATEX*>input_wave_format, null)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass audioClient.Initialize")

        res = input_audioClient.vtable.GetService(input_audioClient, &IAudioCaptureClient_GUID, &captureService)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass audioClient.GetService")

        input_audioClient.vtable.Start(input_audioClient)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass audioClient.Start")

        Assert(input_wave_format.Format.nChannels == 1 || input_wave_format.Format.nChannels == 2)

        is_float := equal(cast<char*>&input_wave_format.SubFormat, cast<char*>&KSDATAFORMAT_SUBTYPE_IEEE_FLOAT, 16)
        Assert(is_float || equal(cast<char*>&input_wave_format.SubFormat, cast<char*>&KSDATAFORMAT_SUBTYPE_PCM, 16))

        input_format.init(input_wave_format.Format.nSamplesPerSec, input_wave_format.Format.nChannels, input_wave_format.Format.wBitsPerSample, is_float)

        // TODO: Extinsible format may have multiple channels, more than SPEAKER_FRONT_LEFT and SPEAKER_FRONT_RIGHT.
        //   Those are the only channels we support so we should zero the other ones (if using float, if using 16-bit then set to 2^15).

        // TODO: Handle 16-bit and 32-bit integer?
        // Assert(input_wave_format.Format.nChannels == 2)
        // Assert(input_wave_format.Format.wBitsPerSample == 32)
        // Assert(input_wave_format.Format.nSamplesPerSec == 48000)
        // Assert(equal(cast<char*>&expected_format.SubFormat, cast<char*>&KSDATAFORMAT_SUBTYPE_PCM, 16))
    }

    fn switchAudioDevice(id: u64) {
        mutex.lock()
        backend_id: StringBuilder
        for internalAudioDevices {
            if it.id == id {
                backend_id.append(it.backend_id)
                break
            }
        }
        mutex.unlock()

        if !backend_id.len {
            // could not find id.
            log("Audio: Could not find audio device ID: ", id)
            sound_state.selected_output_device_id = sound_state.current_output_device_id
            return
        }

        res: HRESULT
        collection: IMMDeviceCollection*
        res = enumerator.vtable.EnumAudioEndpoints(enumerator, eRender, DEVICE_STATE_ACTIVE, &collection)
        CHECK_HR

        count: u32
        res = collection.vtable.GetCount(collection, &count)
        CHECK_HR

        endpoint: IMMDevice*
        for 0..count {
            props: IPropertyStore*
            res = collection.vtable.Item(collection, nr, &endpoint)
            CHECK_HR
            // log("pass Item ",nr)

            res = endpoint.vtable.OpenPropertyStore(endpoint, STGM_READ, &props)
            CHECK_HR
            // log("pass OpenPropertyStore")

            device_id: u16*
            res = endpoint.vtable.GetId(endpoint, &device_id)
            CHECK_HR
        
            device_id_str: StringBuilder
            ParseWideCharString(device_id, &device_id_str)
            // log("Try switch device ",backend_id, " = ", device_id_str)

            if backend_id == device_id_str {
                // we found the one
                props.vtable.Release(props)
                break
            }


            props.vtable.Release(props)
            endpoint.vtable.Release(endpoint)

            endpoint = null
        }

        collection.vtable.Release(collection)

        if endpoint {
            // Release current device
            renderService.vtable.Release(renderService)
            audioClient.vtable.Release(audioClient)
            device.vtable.Release(device)

            // setup new device
            device = endpoint
            init_device()
            sound_state.current_output_device_id = id
        } else {
            // keep using previous device
        }
    }
    
    fn cleanup() {
        res: i32

        audioClient.vtable.Stop(audioClient)
        CHECK_HR
        WASLOG("pass audioClient.Stop")

        renderService.vtable.Release(renderService)
        audioClient.vtable.Release(audioClient)
        device.vtable.Release(device)
        enumerator.vtable.Release(enumerator)
        CoUninitialize()
    }
    // frames that have not been consumed
    fn remainingFrames() -> i32 {
        res: i32
        Assert(audioClient)
        frames: i32
        res = audioClient.vtable.GetCurrentPadding(audioClient, cast<u32*>&frames)
        CHECK_HR
        // log("pass audioClient.GetCurrentPadding")
        return frames
    }
    fn acquireBuffer(frames: i32) -> u8* {
        res: i32
        Assert(renderService)
        renderBuffer: u8*
        res = renderService.vtable.GetBuffer(renderService, frames, &renderBuffer)
        CHECK_HR
        // log("pass renderService.GetBuffer")

        return renderBuffer
    }
    fn releaseBuffer(written_frames: i32) {
        res: i32
        Assert(renderService)
        res = renderService.vtable.ReleaseBuffer(renderService, written_frames, 0)
        CHECK_HR
        // log("pass renderService.ReleaseBuffer")
    }
    fn remainingInputFrames() -> i32 {
        res: i32
        Assert(audioClient)
        frames: i32
        res = audioClient.vtable.GetCurrentPadding(audioClient, cast<u32*>&frames)
        CHECK_HR
        // log("pass audioClient.GetCurrentPadding")
        return frames
    }
    fn acquireInputBuffer(frames: i32) -> u8* {
        res: i32
        Assert(renderService)
        renderBuffer: u8*
        res = renderService.vtable.GetBuffer(renderService, frames, &renderBuffer)
        CHECK_HR
        // log("pass renderService.GetBuffer")

        return renderBuffer
    }
    fn releaseInputBuffer(written_frames: i32) {
        res: i32
        Assert(renderService)
        res = renderService.vtable.ReleaseBuffer(renderService, written_frames, 0)
        CHECK_HR
        // log("pass renderService.ReleaseBuffer")
    }
    
    fn listAudioDevices(list: Array<AudioDeviceInfo>*, exclude_input_devices: bool = false, exclude_output_devices: bool = false) {
        Assert(sound_state)

        res: HRESULT

        collection: IMMDeviceCollection*

        res = enumerator.vtable.EnumAudioEndpoints(enumerator, eRender, DEVICE_STATE_ACTIVE, &collection)
        CHECK_HR

        count: u32
        res = collection.vtable.GetCount(collection, &count)
        CHECK_HR

        default_device: IMMDevice*
        res = enumerator.vtable.GetDefaultAudioEndpoint(enumerator, eRender, eConsole, &default_device)

        default_id: u16*
        if res == 0 {
            res = default_device.vtable.GetId(default_device, &default_id)
            CHECK_HR
        } else if res != cast<i32>E_NOTFOUND {
            CHECK_HR
        }

        for 0..count {
            endpoint: IMMDevice*
            props: IPropertyStore*
            res = collection.vtable.Item(collection, nr, &endpoint)
            CHECK_HR
            // log("pass Item ",nr)

            res = endpoint.vtable.OpenPropertyStore(endpoint, STGM_READ, &props)
            CHECK_HR
            // log("pass OpenPropertyStore")


            ch: char[50] // extra buffer in case PROPVARIANT is defined to small
            prop_name: PROPVARIANT
            

            res = props.vtable.GetValue(props, &DEVPKEY_Device_FriendlyName, &prop_name)
            CHECK_HR
            // log("pass GetValue")

            info := list.add()
            
            ParseWideCharString(cast<u16*>cast<void*>prop_name.union_data, &info.name)


            device_id: u16*
            res = endpoint.vtable.GetId(endpoint, &device_id)
            CHECK_HR
        
            fn equal(a: u16*, b: u16*) -> bool {
                if !a || !b
                    return false
                while *a == *b {
                    if *a == 0 {
                        return true
                    }
                    a+=2
                    b+=2
                }
                return false
            }
            info.is_default = equal(device_id, default_id)

            device_id_str: StringBuilder
            ParseWideCharString(device_id, &device_id_str)
            // log(device_id_str)

            found := false
            for sound_state.audio_backend.internalAudioDevices {
                if it.backend_id == device_id_str {
                    found = true
                    info.id = it.id
                }
            }
            if !found {
                internal_info := sound_state.audio_backend.internalAudioDevices.add()
                internal_info.backend_id.append(device_id_str)
                internal_info.id = sound_state.audio_backend.next_device_id()
                info.id = internal_info.id
            }

            // log("Device ",nr, " : ", name)

            props.vtable.Release(props)
            endpoint.vtable.Release(endpoint)
        }

        collection.vtable.Release(collection)
        default_device.vtable.Release(default_device)
    }
}

// @param path Only WAV audio files are supported (the file does not need .wav extension, format is determined with RIFF and WAVE magic numbers). The SoundRef will be named path.
// @param offset_into_file Useful when reading packed custom game asset files.
// @param stream_file Read chunks from the file as the audio is needed. Recommended for music
//    with a long duration but, stream is not necessary for short sounds with small memory footprint.
//    IMPORTANT: File needs to be readable as long as the SoundRef exists. Otherwise you may play
//      a sound but when new chunks are needed reading from the file fails and then NO AUDIO!
fn LoadSoundFromFile(path: char[], offset_into_file: i64 = 0, stream_file: bool = false) -> SoundRef, SoundError  {
    Assert(sound_state)

    res: i32

    if stream_file {
        Assert(false);
        // TODO: Check valid WAV format
        // TODO: If streaming file then don't read whole file. In fact. don't read it at all.
    } else {
        filesize: i64
        file := FileOpen(path, FILE_READ_ONLY, &filesize)
        if !file {
            return { SOUND_INVALID_HANDLE }, SOUND_FILE_NOT_FOUND
        }
        defer FileClose(file)

        if filesize - offset_into_file < WAVFILE_HEADER_SIZE {
            // file too small
            return { SOUND_INVALID_HANDLE }, SOUND_CORRUPTED_FILE
        }

        res = FileSetHead(file, offset_into_file, FILE_SET_BEG)
        Assert(res == offset_into_file) // TODO: Don't assert

        // NOTE: This is a little complicated since we don't want to read the whole file at once in case
        //   is a huge game asset pack. We first check and read the WAV header and then check and read the data.
        temp_max := 64
        temp := Allocate(temp_max)
        Assert(temp)


        res = FileRead(file, temp, WAVFILE_HEADER_SIZE)
        Assert(res == WAVFILE_HEADER_SIZE)

        wav, err := ParseWAVHeader({temp, WAVFILE_HEADER_SIZE}, path = path, print = true)
        if !wav {
            Free(temp, temp_max)
            return { SOUND_INVALID_HANDLE }, SOUND_CORRUPTED_FILE
        }

        new_temp := Reallocate(wav.size_of_chunks, temp, temp_max)
        Assert(new_temp)

        res = FileRead(file, new_temp, wav.size_of_chunks)
        Assert(res == wav.size_of_chunks)

        ParseWAVChunks({new_temp, wav.size_of_chunks}, wav, use_passed_data_memory = true, path = path, print = true)

        sound := sound_state.createSoundRef(path) // path is name of sound ref
        sound.wav = wav
        sound.format = wav.format
        sound.total_frames = wav.data_len / cast<i32>wav.block_align
        
        ref: SoundRef
        ref.handle = sound.handle
        return ref, SOUND_SUCCESS
    }
    return { SOUND_INVALID_HANDLE }, SOUND_UNSUPPORTED_FILE_FORMAT
}
fn CreateSoundStream(name: char[], buffer_size: i32, format: AudioFormat*) -> SoundRef, SoundError {
    Assert(sound_state)

    // size must be a multiple of frame size. We can't have 57.23 samples in the buffer, it must be a whole number.
    Assert(buffer_size % format.get_frame_size() == 0)

    sound := sound_state.createSoundRef(name)

    sound.format = *format
    sound.buffer.ptr = Allocate(buffer_size)
    sound.buffer.len = buffer_size
    Assert(sound.buffer.ptr)

    ref: SoundRef
    ref.handle = sound.handle
    return ref, SOUND_SUCCESS
}
// @param name SoundRef will get this name.
// @param file_data Raw file data to parse (only .wav files are supported). Useful if you store assets in one large packed file. With LoadSoundFromFile you are limited to the OS file system.
// @param stream_file TODO: Not sure if this should exist? It requires the file_data memory to continously exist.
fn LoadSoundFromRawFileData(name: char[], file_data: u8[], stream_file: bool = false) -> SoundRef, SoundError  {
    Assert(sound_state)

    if stream_file {
        // TODO: Check valid WAV format
        // TODO: Implement streaming
        Assert(false);
    } else {
        // TODO: Look at LoadSoundFromFile
        Assert(false);
    }
    
    return { SOUND_INVALID_HANDLE }, SOUND_UNSUPPORTED_FILE_FORMAT
}

/*######################
        WAV File
#######################*/

enum WAVError {
    WAV_SUCCESS,
    WAV_CORRUPT,
    WAV_FILE_NOT_FOUND,
}

#macro WAVFILE_HEADER_SIZE 36

#macro WAVFILE_AUDIO_FORMAT_PCM_INTEGER 1
#macro WAVFILE_AUDIO_FORMAT_IEEE_FLOAT 3

struct WAVFile {
    // IMPORTANT: IF YOU CHANGE STUFF HERE YOU NEED TO CHANGE STUFF IN EXAMPLES. One of them is: sample_wasapi.btb 
    audio_format: u16;
    channels: u16;
    sample_rate: u32;
    byte_rate: u32;
    block_align: u16; // bits_per_sample * channels
    bits_per_sample: u16;

    format: AudioFormat;

    size_of_chunks: i32; // size of the remaining chunks (size of whole file except for header)

    owner_of_data_allocation: bool; // if true then you need to free data
    data: u8*;
    data_len: i32;
}

// TODO: Argument to pass in allocator
// @param path only used when printing error
fn ParseWAVHeader(data: u8[], print: bool = false, path: char[] = "unknown") -> WAVFile*, WAVError {
    head := 0
    char_ptr := cast<char*>data.ptr

    if data.len < WAVFILE_HEADER_SIZE {
        if print
            log("ParseWAVHeader: Size of data is too small for WAV header (was ",data.len, " needs to be at least ",WAVFILE_HEADER_SIZE,"), origin path = ",path)
        return null, WAV_CORRUPT
    }
    
    if !equal(char_ptr + head, "RIFF".ptr, 4) {
        if print
            log("ParseWAVHeader: 'RIFF' magic incorrect (was ",Slice<char>{char_ptr+head,4},"), origin path = ",path)
        return null, WAV_CORRUPT
    }
    head += 4

    almost_file_size := *cast<i32*>(data.ptr + head)
    head += 4

    // if data.len < almost_file_size + 8 {
    //     if print
    //         log("ParseWAVHeader: File size in header was ",almost_file_size," bytes which does not match size of passed in data of ",data.len,"-8 (-8 because RIFF and file size field are excluded), origin path = ",path)
    //     return null, WAV_CORRUPT
    // }

    if !equal(char_ptr + head, "WAVE".ptr, 4) {
        if print
            log("ParseWAVHeader: 'WAVE' marker incorrect (was ",Slice<char>{char_ptr+head,4},"), origin path = ",path)
        return null, WAV_CORRUPT
    }
    head += 4

    // TODO: Can WAV files have multiple format and data chunks? If so handle it.

    if !equal(char_ptr + head, "fmt ".ptr, 4) {
        if print
            log("ParseWAVHeader: 'fmt ' marker incorrect (was ",Slice<char>{char_ptr+head,4},"), origin path = ",path)
        return null, WAV_CORRUPT
    }
    head += 4

    format_size := *cast<i32*>(data.ptr + head)
    head += 4

    if format_size != 16 { // always 16 for WAVE (i think)
        if print
            log("ParseWAVHeader: Size of format was ",format_size," bytes but should be 16 (probably?), origin path = ",path)
        return null, WAV_CORRUPT
    }

    success := false
    wav: WAVFile* = Allocate(sizeof(WAVFile))
    construct(wav)
    defer if !success DestroyWAVFile(wav)
    
    wav.size_of_chunks = almost_file_size - WAVFILE_HEADER_SIZE + 8

    wav.audio_format    = *cast<u16*>(data.ptr + head)
    wav.channels        = *cast<u16*>(data.ptr + head + 2)
    wav.sample_rate     = *cast<u32*>(data.ptr + head + 4)
    wav.byte_rate       = *cast<u32*>(data.ptr + head + 8)
    wav.block_align     = *cast<u16*>(data.ptr + head + 12)
    wav.bits_per_sample = *cast<u16*>(data.ptr + head + 14)
    head += 16

    wav.format.init(wav.sample_rate, wav.channels, wav.bits_per_sample, wav.audio_format == WAVFILE_AUDIO_FORMAT_IEEE_FLOAT)

    Assert(wav.audio_format == WAVFILE_AUDIO_FORMAT_IEEE_FLOAT || wav.audio_format == WAVFILE_AUDIO_FORMAT_PCM_INTEGER)

    Assert(head == WAVFILE_HEADER_SIZE)

    success = true // so we don't defer and destroy WAV file
    return wav, WAV_SUCCESS
}
// If error is returned don't forget to destroy WAVFile returned by ParseWAVHeader
// @param data slice should directly point at data section in WAV file (WAVFILE_HEADER_SIZE from start)
fn ParseWAVChunks(data: u8[], wav: WAVFile*, use_passed_data_memory: bool = false, print: bool = false, path: char[] = "unknown") -> WAVError {
    head: u32 = 0
    char_ptr := cast<char*>data.ptr
    while head < cast<u32>data.len {
        chunk_id := char_ptr + head
        head += 4
        chunk_len := *cast<u32*>(data.ptr + head)
        head += 4

        // log(Slice<char>{chunk_id,4}, " ", chunk_len)

        if equal(chunk_id, "data".ptr, 4) {
            if cast<u32>data.len - head < chunk_len {
                if print
                    log("ParseWAVChunks: Data chunk size was ",chunk_len," bytes which is to large, expected no less than ",data.len-head," bytes which is the remaining size of the file data, origin path = ",path)
                return WAV_CORRUPT
            }

            wav.data_len = chunk_len

            if(cast<u32>wav.data_len % wav.block_align != 0) {
                if print
                    log("ParseWAVChunks: Size of data should be divisible by block_align (",wav.data_len," % ",wav.block_align,"=",(cast<u32>wav.data_len%wav.block_align),"), origin path = ",path)
                return WAV_CORRUPT
            }

            if use_passed_data_memory {
                wav.data = data.ptr + head
            } else {
                wav.data = Allocate(chunk_len)
                Assert(wav.data)
                memcpy(wav.data, data.ptr + head, chunk_len)
                wav.owner_of_data_allocation = true
            }

            head += chunk_len
        } else {
            // skip unknown chunk
            head += chunk_len
        }
    }

    if !wav.data {
        if print
            log("ParseWAVChunks: 'data' chunk does not exist, origin path = ",path)
        return WAV_CORRUPT
    }

    return WAV_SUCCESS
}

fn DestroyWAVFile(wav: WAVFile*) {
    if wav.data
        Free(wav.data, wav.data_len)
    Free(wav, sizeof(WAVFile))
}

// IMPORTANT: DO NOT MODIFY THE SIGNATURE OR NAME OF THIS FUNCTION. It is used by examples (sample_wasapi.btb).
//   You can change the internals as long as the function returns WAVFile with WAV data.
fn ReadWAVFile(path: char[], print: bool = false) -> WAVFile*, WAVError {
    data, yes := ReadWholeFile(path)
    if !yes {
        if print
            log("ReadWAVFile: Could not read ", path)
        return null, WAV_FILE_NOT_FOUND
    }
    wav, err := ParseWAVHeader(Slice<u8>{cast<u8*>data.ptr, data.len}, print, path)
    if wav {
        err = ParseWAVData(Slice<u8>{cast<u8*>data.ptr + WAVFILE_HEADER_SIZE, data.len - WAVFILE_HEADER_SIZE}, wav, print, path)
        if err != WAV_SUCCESS {
            DestroyWAVFile(wav)
        }
    }
    return wav, err
}

fn WriteWAVFile(path: char[], format: AudioFormat*, audio_data: u8[]) -> bool {
    file := FileOpen(path, FILE_CLEAR_AND_WRITE)
    if !file
        return false
    defer FileClose(file)
    
    almost_file_size: i32 = WAVFILE_HEADER_SIZE - 8 + 8 + audio_data.len

    FileWrite(file, "RIFF".ptr, 4)
    FileWrite(file, &almost_file_size, 4)
    FileWrite(file, "WAVE".ptr, 4)
    FileWrite(file, "fmt ".ptr, 4)
    tmp: i32 = 16
    FileWrite(file, &tmp, 4)

    form: WAVFile
    if format.sample_format == AUDIO_32BIT_FLOAT
        form.audio_format = WAVFILE_AUDIO_FORMAT_IEEE_FLOAT
    else
        form.audio_format = WAVFILE_AUDIO_FORMAT_PCM_INTEGER
    form.channels = format.get_channels()
    form.sample_rate = format.sample_rate
    form.byte_rate = format.sample_rate * format.get_frame_size()
    form.block_align = format.get_frame_size()
    form.bits_per_sample = format.get_bits_per_sample()

    FileWrite(file, &form.audio_format, 2)
    FileWrite(file, &form.channels, 2)
    FileWrite(file, &form.sample_rate, 4)
    FileWrite(file, &form.byte_rate, 4)
    FileWrite(file, &form.block_align, 2)
    FileWrite(file, &form.bits_per_sample, 2)

    FileWrite(file, "data".ptr, 4)
    tmp = audio_data.len
    FileWrite(file, &tmp, 4)

    FileWrite(file, audio_data.ptr, audio_data.len)
    return true
}


/*###############################
     Implementation and design
#################################

The Sound module has two parts (not clearly seperated). The audio backend, which uses WASAPI for Windows and ALSA for Linux, and the audio system which provides a platform independent API for developers to play sounds, capture audio, stream music from a file, start and pause sounds, and play 3D sounds.

Behind the scenes the audio system talks to the backend and provides it with audio data, enumerates devices, retrives microphone audio, reads audio from sound files in various WAVE formats, mixes multiple sounds based on volume, pitch, 3D location (if enabled for the particular sound), processes intense mixing in a seperate thread if you enable it or if you want to call the process sound function yourself.


#########################
        Questions?
#########################


# WHY SoundRef AND SoundInstance

We have SoundRef and SoundInstance because you may want to load a item collect sound
and play it simultaneously. If we just had SoundRef you would load the sound and call play
but what happens if you call play again? Do we stop the current sound that is playing, well no.
Sure we could internal create a new instance of the sound but from a developers view it's just SoundRef.
Problem is that the sound may be a NPC voice which you want to pause when player presses escape.
To pause the sound we need to know which sound we're pausing and we may not want to pause all sounds.

Ultimately we have SoundRef.play_once() for coin collect sounds and SoundRef.create_instance() with more
control for NPC voice sound.

# WHY SoundRef and SoundRef_impl

This is not about hiding implementation details (if I cared about that structs would have private and public fields).
The reasoning behind this is SoundRef not being a pointer meaning that you can call cleanup on it and the data in SoundRef
will be reset. Next time you call a method on it will Assert or do nothing.

With a pointer where allocate and free it you may call cleanup and forget to throwaway the pointer. By having SoundRef as non-pointer that refers to some allocated memory for SoundRef_impl we have more control and options and less error prone.

# WHY ONLY WAV?

Audio files are complex and hard to implement and the documentation on them is somewhat lacking. Therefore you need to use a library for them. But also, I doubt we would be able to implement a better ogg or mp3 decoder than what popular libraries do 
so you probably want to use a library anyway.

# WHY IS 0 as SoundRef.handle INVALID?

Over the years I have made systems use 0 as handle which is problematic since it is a valid index.
Either you have an array where the first element is unused or when accessing the array you do 'handle-1' and
when creating handles 'handle+1'. This is awful to keep track of so unused first element is best. I have also used -1
to indicate invalid handle which is problematic because you can't reset structs, elements and arrays which contain handles
to zero since that means a valid handle.

A minor or major issue with the language is x: u64 = -1 becomes 4294967295 not -1 which isn't great. It happens because literal integers are i32 by default.
The type of literals are not inferred which they should be.

This time I have chosen 0 as invalid handle and first element being unused if handle is an index into an array. (we may use a hashmap instead)

*/

























